const body = `<h1>9.4b — Implementing Interfaces: Multiple Behaviors</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Multi-Ability Character</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you saw that interfaces define contracts. A class can implement multiple interfaces — something inheritance alone can't do. You'll build a character that can attack AND heal AND defend.</p>
        <p>Create three interfaces:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><code>Attacker</code>: method <code>int attack()</code></li>
            <li><code>Healer</code>: method <code>int heal()</code></li>
            <li><code>Defender</code>: method <code>int defend(int incomingDamage)</code></li>
        </ol>
        <p>Create <code>Paladin</code> that implements ALL THREE:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><code>attack()</code>: deals 15 damage.</li>
            <li><code>heal()</code>: restores 10 HP.</li>
            <li><code>defend(int dmg)</code>: reduces damage by 8.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Create <code>Priest</code> that implements Healer and Defender only (no attack).</p>
        <p>Test by storing a Paladin in each interface type — what methods are available?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/object-interfaces-casting.html">Interfaces</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
